.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt CMC 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cmc
.Nd the libcmc model: programs, worlds, the environment and its rules
.Sh DESCRIPTION
libcmc is an explicit-state model checker for protocol code written in
C.
It checks the program you ship: each node is that program, run
unmodified as a process, and the checker is the network, the clock and
the crash between them.
It explores every interleaving of connections, messages, timeouts,
crashes and lost messages, up to a bound, and checks invariants in
every state it reaches.
A model file says which program, which environment and which
invariants; it carries no behaviour.
.Pp
The engine has a second front end, in-process step functions
.Pq Vt CmcCore ,
which is how it is calibrated against TLC; that model is described from
.Sx Cores and worlds
on.
.Xr cmc 1
gives the command line, and
.Xr libcmc 3
the C interface.
.Ss Programs as nodes
.Nm cmc Fl -model-file
starts one process per node, the same program each time, with the node
number as its first argument.
Each runs under seccomp user notification and under
.Pa cmc-preload.so ,
which gives it a heap at fixed addresses and routes the clock and
.Fn getrandom
to the checker.
The checker answers every
.Fn socket ,
.Fn bind ,
.Fn listen ,
.Fn connect ,
.Fn accept ,
.Fn read ,
.Fn write ,
.Fn close ,
.Fn poll
.Pf ( Fn ppoll ) ,
.Fn fcntl
and
.Fn eventfd
on a connection itself: no real socket forms.
An
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
fd set with
.Fn fcntl
reads
.Dv EAGAIN
when nothing is deliverable, so an event loop loops instead of
blocking;
.Fn eventfd
is a counter the loop wakes itself with.
Node
.Va i
listens on 127.0.0.1 port 20000 +
.Va i ,
and a connection goes to the node whose port was dialled.
.Pp
A node that calls
.Fn accept ,
reads a connection with nothing the search has chosen to deliver, or
calls
.Fn poll
with no fd locally ready, stays blocked in the kernel.
A
.Fn poll
over an
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
socket is a readiness query: a requested
.Dv POLLOUT
and a non-zero
.Fn eventfd
answer at once, but a connection or listener is ready only when the
search says so, exactly as a deferred read is.
When every node is blocked or has exited the world is
.Em quiescent :
the checker reads each node's state, checks the invariants, and the
enabled actions are the next steps:
.Bl -tag -width "TIMEOUT" -compact
.It ACCEPT
A node in
.Fn accept
takes a connection waiting at it.
.It DELIVER
A node reading a connection gets the oldest message on it; each
connection is FIFO, as TCP is.
.It EOF
The peer is gone and the connection is drained: the read returns 0.
.It TIMEOUT
Timeout budget is left: a node waiting for input has its timer fire.
A node reading a connection gets the one byte
.Ql T
instead of a message; a node idle in
.Fn accept
accepts a synthetic timer connection and reads
.Ql T
from it.
Either way the logical clock advances one second.
This is how a server that waits for a request, or for a peer to connect,
detects that it has waited long enough -- an election timeout.
.It CRASH
Crash budget is left: a live node dies.
.It DROP
Drop budget is left: the oldest message on a connection is lost.
.It WAKE
A node parked in
.Fn poll
is told one polled fd is ready.
It takes no message: the node then
.Fn accept Ns s
or reads that fd and blocks again, which ACCEPT, DELIVER or TIMEOUT
services, so poll readiness stays a search decision.
.El
.Pp
A node's state is what it reports.
After each change it writes a canonical encoding of its logical state,
a length byte then the bytes, to file descriptor 3; what it has made
durable it writes the same way to descriptor 193.
A crash keeps only the durable encoding.
The encoding must be canonical (the same logical state, the same bytes)
and complete (everything the node's future depends on, its position in
its own code included); anything left out lets the checker merge two
states that differ, and miss a bug.
.Pp
The world is every node's state, liveness and what it is blocked in,
its open connections with each one's
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
flag, the polled fd set of a node parked in
.Fn poll ,
every
.Fn eventfd
counter, every connection's messages in order, the connections waiting
to be accepted, the ghost log of client replies, the budgets left and
the logical clock.
Its 128-bit fingerprint dedups states.
The search is stateless: to expand a world the checker starts every
node again and replays the actions that reached it.
Breadth-first search gives a shortest counterexample; simulation takes
seeded random walks and is never a pass.
.Ss Cores and worlds
A
.Em core
is a vtable over a fixed-size, pointer-free state per node.
The engine treats that state as opaque bytes.
A
.Em world
is every node's state followed by the environment the engine owns:
.Bl -tag -width "persists" -compact
.It node states
The first
.Va durable_size
bytes of each are durable.
.It header
Remaining budgets; per node, the clients left, armed timers, pending
wakes, pending persists and pending connection resets.
.It network
The messages in flight.
.It persists
Each node's pending disk writes, oldest first.
.It replies
The ghost log of client replies, in emission order.
.El
.Pp
Every part is kept canonical, so two worlds are the same state exactly
when their bytes are equal.
The engine hashes the bytes into a 128-bit fingerprint and stores the
fingerprints of the states it has seen.
.Ss Where actions come from
In a message-passing core, an action becomes possible only through
something a core emitted, or through an environment budget.
The engine never reads a core's state to decide what can happen next:
.Bl -tag -width "CRASH(n, j)" -compact
.It DELIVER
A message is in flight (FIFO: only the head of its channel).
.It DROP
Drop budget is left.
In FIFO mode a drop resets the connection.
.It DUP
Dup budget is left; bag mode only.
.It FIRE(n, t)
Node n armed timer t and has not cancelled it.
.It FLUSH(n)
Node n has a pending persist; the oldest completes first.
.It WAKE(n, w)
Node n emitted a wake of waiter w.
.It CLIENT(n)
Node n has client budget left.
.It CRASH(n, j)
Crash budget is left; the first j of the node's pending persists reach
disk.
.It RESET(n, p)
A crash or a drop left node n a pending reset of its connection to p.
.El
.Pp
An action is one call to the node's
.Fn step
with the matching event.
The engine then queues the sends, arms or cancels the timers, queues the
persists and wakes, and appends the replies.
.Pp
This rule is what separates libcmc from a simulator that consults the
system under test: an oracle that reads a core's state to schedule it
can hide exactly the bugs, such as lost wake-ups, that the check exists
to find.
Properties may read core state; the code that computes possible actions
never calls them.
.Ss Durability and crashes
The engine owns the durable bytes.
On FLUSH it applies the oldest pending record through the core's
.Fn apply_persist ,
then delivers
.Dv CMC_EV_FLUSHED
to
.Fn step .
.Fn step
must leave the durable bytes unchanged on every event; the engine
compares them before and after each call.
.Pp
CRASH(n, j) applies the first j pending records, zeroes the volatile
bytes, the timers, the wakes and the remaining persists, empties every
channel to and from n, marks a reset pending at each peer, and delivers
.Dv CMC_EV_RESTART .
Branching over j covers a write that reached disk before its completion
was observed.
.Ss Network
.Bl -tag -width "fifo" -compact
.It fifo
The default: one ordered channel per pair of nodes, as with TCP.
It never duplicates.
.It bag
An unordered multiset that may duplicate, for transports without order.
It allows more behaviour than FIFO, so a counterexample found in bag
mode may be spurious for a protocol that relies on FIFO order.
.El
.Ss Search
Breadth-first search, the default, has the semantics of TLC run with
one worker: a shortest counterexample, a depth that counts the initial
state as 1, and exact counts of distinct and generated states.
Depth-first search stops at a depth bound and reports a search that hit
it as incomplete.
Simulation runs seeded random walks.
Replay runs a saved counterexample through the real cores.
.Ss Calibration mode
A TLA+ specification of shared registers has no messages.
A core that ports one sets
.Fn direct_actions :
the world is then one state, and the possible actions are the
specification's
.Sy Next
disjuncts, applied through
.Dv CMC_EV_ACTION .
Only calibration cores do this.
The engine is calibrated this way against TLC on 22 configurations of a
published consensus specification, and must give the same verdicts,
counterexample depths and distinct-state counts.
.Sh RULES
These are the rules that keep a pass meaningful.
A change to the engine that breaks one is a bug, whatever else it
improves.
For the process front end they read: actions come only from what the
programs did (dialled, wrote, blocked) and from budgets; the world holds
everything the future depends on; a crash keeps only what a node
declared durable; a bound, a cap, an unknown model-file key, a missing
preload or a node silent in a call the checker does not model is an
error or an incomplete search, never a pass.
.Bl -enum
.It
No omniscient oracle.
Actions are enabled only by what a core emitted or by a budget.
.It
The world is the state.
Everything that affects future behaviour lives in the world bytes, kept
canonical, and nothing else does: not padding, not uninitialised bytes,
not a global in the core.
.It
The engine owns durability.
Only
.Fn apply_persist
changes durable bytes, and a crash may keep any prefix of the pending
persists.
.It
Limits are errors.
Going past a cap is reported as a violation, never truncated.
.It
TLC semantics.
Breadth-first search gives shortest counterexamples and exact counts,
and the calibration rows match TLC.
.El
.Sh GATES
.Bl -tag -width "purity gate"
.It Fl -det
Runs every step twice from copies of the same state and compares the
outputs and the next states: a non-deterministic core fails.
.It purity gate
.Pa scripts/purity_gate.sh
inspects a core's object files and sources: no calls but
.Fn mem* ,
no writable or thread-local data, no clock, randomness, system-call or
CPU-identification instructions, no
.Ql asm ,
and no direct writes to
.Fa out->o .
.Pa scripts/purity_teeth.sh
checks that the gate rejects a probe for each of these.
.It MemorySanitizer
Uninitialised reads, which
.Fl -det
cannot see.
.It planted bugs
Each
.Ql #if
bug site in a core's shipped source must be caught, with the expected
property, by search, by simulation and by replay.
.It calibration
The engine itself, against TLC.
.El
.Sh LIMITS
Process front end: Linux x86-64; at most 8 nodes, 64 bytes per write,
128 messages in flight, 16 connections per node, 64 client replies, 64
bytes of reported state per node, and 255 for each budget.
A target must be built
.Fl no-pie ,
use the port convention, and do its I/O through the calls above; a node
that blocks in anything else for 10 seconds is an error.
A read gets at most one write, a connect never fails, and
.Fn close
does not notify the peer.
.Pp
In-process cores: at most 8 nodes, 32 bytes per message or persist
record, 16 effects per step, 2048 possible actions per state, 16 timers
and 8 waiters per node, 256 bytes of core settings, and 255 for each
budget and cap.
